WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:09.240
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
episode number 500 with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.500
And on this glorious day, we are going to
simulate the kinematics of yarn and cloth

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.600
on our computers.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.000
We will transition into today’s paper in
a moment, but for context, here is a wonderful

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.490
work to show you what we were able to do in
2012 and we will see how far we have come

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:29.490
since.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.319
This previous work was about creating these
highly detailed cloth geometries for digital

00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:35.859
characters.

00:00:35.859 --> 00:00:41.479
Here you see one of its coolest results where
it shows how the simulated forces pull the

00:00:41.479 --> 00:00:44.190
entire piece of garment together.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:50.329
We start out with dreaming up a piece of cloth
geometry, and this simulator gradually transforms

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:56.309
it into a real-world version of that by subjecting
it to real physical forces.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:59.049
This is a step that we call yarn-level relaxation.

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:05.479
A few years ago, when I worked at Disney Research,
I attended to the talk of the Oscar award

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:09.700
winning researcher Steve Marschner who presented
this paper.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.820
And when I saw these results, shockwaves went
through my body.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.140
It was one of my truly formative “hold on
to your papers” moments that I’ll never

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.229
forget.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:24.079
Now note that to produce these results, one
had to wait for hours and hours to compute

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:25.590
all these interactions.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:33.270
So, this paper was published in 2012, and
now, nearly 9 years have passed, so I wonder

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.539
how far have come some since?

00:01:35.539 --> 00:01:37.570
Well, let’s see together!

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:42.719
Today, with this new technique, we can conjure
up similar animations where pieces of garments

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:43.719
tighten.

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:44.719
Beautiful.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:51.659
Now, let’s look under the hood of the simulator,
and…well, well, well.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:54.039
Do you see what I see here?

00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:55.090
Red dots.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.510
So, why did I get so excited about a couple
red dots?

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.670
Let’s find out together!

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.929
These red dots solve a fundamental problem
when simulating the movement of these yarns.

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:11.950
The issue is that in the mathematical description
of this problem, there is a stiffness term

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:17.110
that does not behave well when two of these
points slide too close to each other.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:22.410
Interestingly, our simulated material gets
infinitely stiff in these points.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:27.230
This is incorrect behavior and it makes the
simulation unstable.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.550
Not good.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.380
So what do we to to alleviate this?

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:37.200
Now, we can use this new technique that detects
these cases and addresses them by introducing

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.710
these additional red nodes.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.520
These are used as a stand-in until things
stabilize.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.760
Look, we wait until these two points slide
off of each other.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.880
And now, the distances are large enough so
that the mathematical framework can regain

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:00.720
its validity and compute the stiffness term
correctly, and look, the red dot disappears,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.540
and the simulation can continue without breaking.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:10.070
So if we go back to another piece of under
the hood footage, we now understand why these

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.470
red dots come and go.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:17.170
They come when two nodes get too close to
each other, and they disappear as they pass

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.910
each other, keeping the simulation intact.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.410
And with this method, we can simulate this
beautiful phenomenon when we throw a piece

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:31.590
of garment on the sphere, and all kinds of
stretching and sliding takes place.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.940
Marvelous.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.950
So what else can this do?

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:40.460
Oh boy, it can even simulate multiple cloth
layers, look at the pocket and the stitching

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.150
patterns here.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.190
Beautiful.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:49.250
We can also put a neck tag on this shirt and
start stretching and shearing it into oblivion.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.870
Pay special attention to the difference in
how the shirt and the neck tag reacts to the

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.260
same forces.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:02.010
We can also stack three tablecloths on top
of each other and see how they would behave

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.650
if we would not simulate friction.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:11.430
And now, the same footage with friction.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:12.430
Much more realistic.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:18.440
And if we look under the hood, you see that
the algorithm is doing a ton of work with

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.940
these red nodes.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:27.900
Look, the table notes that they had to insert
tens of thousands of these nodes to keep the

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.410
simulation intact.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:30.540
Goodness!

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.200
So, how long do we have to wait for a simulation
like this?

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.470
The 2012 paper took several hours, what about
this one?

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.940
Well, this says we need a few seconds per
timestep, and typically, several timesteps

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.090
correspond to one frame, so where does this
put us?

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:56.760
Well, it puts us in the domain of not hours
per every frame of animation here, but to

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.460
minutes, and sometimes even seconds per frame.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:05.450
And not only that, but this simulator is also
more robust as it can deal with these unpleasant

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:08.780
cases where these points get too close to
each other.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:14.650
So, I think this was a great testament to
the amazing rate of progress in computer graphics

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:15.730
research.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:06:18.490
What a time to be alive!

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:22.110
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

